   As I remember, changing attunement is not necessarily automatic.  If
   your high-level monster is busy mauling the player right in front of
   him, he may ignore incoming arrow from the opposite end of the room.
   If your worried that Mister Monster won't do anything because maulee
   is inactive, perhaps "attunement" can be defined as follows: monster
   may attune to last to hit him, but still chooses most sensible target.

Picture the following scenario. Two mid-level characters A and B, both
equipped with long-range weapons come upon a strong monster M with
only short range weapons.

M is in the middle between A and B, a few spaces from each. A shoots
M. M attunes to A. What does M do ? Clearly either attack A or B.

Lets say it walks towards A to attack him. B does nothing and A
continues shooting M until M is one field away. Now B shoots M. M
attunes to B. What does M do ?

Try to continue to follow A ? Bad idea. Before B makes another move ,
A gets as many moves as he wants to. He can flee out of the room, or
even onto another level. M can never catch up with him.

Turn and attack B instead ? Bad idea, too. As M walks the other way
toward B and gets shoot again and again by B, A does nothing until M
is one field from B. Now A shoots M a.s.o.

You see ? Without any special powers or items (except sling stone
e.g.) any 2 players can kill any arbitrarily powerful monster (as long
as it does have long-range-weapons, and even then altough that
requires a slightly more complicated strategy).

